\label{prop_d2xi}
Let $X=(\mathcal V,\mathcal A,\mathcal F)$ be a periodic planar graph with bounded geometry. 
Then $\mathscr U_\mathcal A \mathrm d_0\mathscr U_\mathcal V^*$ is the multiplication operator from $L^2(\mathbb{T}^2,\mathbb{C}^\mathscr{v})$ to $L^2(\mathbb{T}^2,\mathbb{C}^\mathscr{e})$ 
by the real-analytic matrix-valued function $\mathrm d_0(\cdot)\colon \mathbb{T}^2\to M_{\mathscr{e}\times \mathscr{v}}(\mathbb{C})$
defined for $\xi \in \mathbb{T}^2$ by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:d0xi}
    \mathrm d_0(\xi)_{ij}=\begin{cases}
        0 &\text{ if } o(\mathfrak e_i)\neq \mathfrak x_j \neq t(\mathfrak e_i),\\
        -1 &\text{ if } o(\mathfrak e_i)= \mathfrak x_j \neq t(\mathfrak e_i),\\
        e^{2\pi i \xi\cdot\eta (\mathfrak e_i) } &\text{ if } o(\mathfrak e_i)\neq \mathfrak x_j = t(\mathfrak e_i),\\
        -1 + e^{2\pi i \xi\cdot\eta (\mathfrak e_i) } &\text{ if } o(\mathfrak e_i)= \mathfrak x_j = t(\mathfrak e_i).
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}

Let us now consider the example of the Kagome lattice given in Figure \ref{fig:kagome_and_quotient} with the help of Figure \ref{fig:fundamentalkagome}. First, consider $\mathrm d_0(\xi)\in M_{6\times 3}(\mathbb{C})$ where each $i$-th line 
    gives the incident information for $\mathfrak e_i$:

    \begin{equation}
\mathrm d_0(\xi)=\begin{pmatrix}                 
-1&1&0\\
-1&e^{-2\pi i\xi_1}&0\\
-1&0&1\\
-1&0&e^{-2\pi i\xi_2}\\
0&-1&1\\
0&-1&e^{2\pi i(\xi_1-\xi_2)}
\end{pmatrix}\ .\label{eq:d0Kagome}
\end{equation}
Moreover, $\mathrm d_1(\xi)\in M_{3\times 6}(\mathbb{C})$ where each $i$-th line gives the incident information for $\mathfrak f_i$ in terms of oriented edges:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm d_1(\xi)=\begin{pmatrix}
-e^{2\pi i(\xi_2-\xi_1)}& -1 & 1 & e^{2\pi i(\xi_2-\xi_1)} & -e^{-2\pi i\xi_1} & -e^{2\pi i(\xi_2-\xi_1)} \\
1&0 & -1 & 0 & 1&0\\
0&e^{2\pi i\xi_1} & 0 & -e^{2\pi i\xi_1} & 0 & 1 
\end{pmatrix}\label{eq:d1Kagome}
    \end{equation}

\label{lem_k3}
For $\xi \in \mathbb{T}^2$, the characteristic polynomial of $D_m(\xi)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}\label{eq_cara_2}
\det\big(D_m(\xi)-\lambda I_{\mathscr{v}+\mathscr{e}+\mathscr{f}} \big) 
= \det \Big((\lambda^{2}-m^2)I_\mathscr{e} - \mathrm d_0(\xi)\mathrm d_0(\xi)^*-\mathrm d_1(\xi)^*\mathrm d_1(\xi)\Big). 
\end{equation}

By the block determinant formula, one gets for $\lambda \neq m$
\begin{align*}
&\det\big(D_m(\xi)-\lambda I_{\mathscr{v}+\mathscr{e}+\mathscr{f}} \big)\\
& =\det \begin{pmatrix}
(m-\lambda) \, I_\mathscr{v} & \mathrm d_0(\xi)^{*} & 0\\
\mathrm d_0(\xi) & (-m-\lambda) \, I_\mathscr{e} &\mathrm d_1(\xi)^* \\
0 & \mathrm d_1(\xi) & (m-\lambda) \, I_\mathscr{f}
\end{pmatrix} \\
& = \det \big((m-\lambda) \, I_\mathscr{f}\big)  \det\left(\begin{pmatrix}
(m-\lambda) \, I_\mathscr{v} & \mathrm d_0(\xi)^{*} \\
\mathrm d_0(\xi) & (-m-\lambda) \, I_\mathscr{e}
\end{pmatrix}
- (m-\lambda)^{-1} \begin{pmatrix}
0 & 0\\
0 &\mathrm d_1(\xi)^*\mathrm d_1(\xi)
\end{pmatrix}\right) \\
& = (m-\lambda)^\mathscr{f}
\ \det\begin{pmatrix}
(m-\lambda) \, I_\mathscr{v} & \mathrm d_0(\xi)^{*} \\
\mathrm d_0(\xi) & (-m-\lambda) \, I_\mathscr{e} - (m-\lambda)^{-1}\mathrm d_1(\xi)^*\mathrm d_1(\xi)
\end{pmatrix} \\
& = (m-\lambda)^{\mathscr{f}+\mathscr{v}}
\det \big(
(-m-\lambda) \, I_\mathscr{e} - (m-\lambda)^{-1}\mathrm d_1(\xi)^*\mathrm d_1(\xi)
- (m-\lambda)^{-1}\mathrm d_0(\xi)\mathrm d_0(\xi)^*\big) \\
& = (m-\lambda)^{\mathscr{f}+\mathscr{v}-\mathscr{e}}\,\det \bigl((\lambda^{2}-m^2)I_\mathscr{e} - \mathrm d_0(\xi)\mathrm d_0(\xi)^*-\mathrm d_1(\xi)^*\mathrm d_1(\xi)\bigr).
\end{align*} 
By taking \eqref{eq:euler0} into account, the first factor vanishes. 
Finally, since both sides are polynomials in $\lambda$, the formula also holds for $\lambda=m$ by continuity.

\label{prop:spectrumduality}
For any $\xi\in\mathbb{T}^2$ one has
\begin{equation}\label{eq:spectrumduality}
\sigma\big(-\Delta_\mathcal A(\xi)\big)=\sigma\big(-\Delta_\mathcal V(\xi)\big)\cup\sigma\big(-\Delta_\mathcal F(\xi)\big).
\end{equation}

The two key observations for this proof is that the operator
$\mathrm d_0(\xi)\mathrm d_0(\xi)^*$ acts non-trivially uniquely in the first entry of the direct sum in \eqref{eq_decHf}
while the operator $\mathrm d_1(\xi)^*\mathrm d_1(\xi)$ acts non-trivially only in the third entry of the direct sum. 
By the standard relation between the spectra of $BB^*$ and $B^*B$ for any matrix $B$, one then gets
\begin{align}\label{eq_with0}
\begin{split}
\sigma\big(-\Delta_\mathcal A(\xi)\big)\setminus \{0\}
=&\Big(\sigma\big(\mathrm d_0(\xi)\mathrm d_0(\xi)^*\big)\cup\sigma\big(\mathrm d_1(\xi)^*\mathrm d_1(\xi)\big)\Big)\setminus\{0\}\\
=&\Big(\sigma\big(\mathrm d_0(\xi)^*\mathrm d_0(\xi)\big)\cup\sigma\big(\mathrm d_1(\xi)\mathrm d_1(\xi)^*\big)\Big)\setminus\{0\}\\
=&\Big(\sigma\big(-\Delta_\mathcal V(\xi)\big)\cup\sigma\big(-\Delta_\mathcal F(\xi)\big)\Big)\setminus\{0\}.
\end{split}
\end{align}

Now, if we assume that $\operatorname{Ker}\big(-\Delta_\mathcal A(\xi)\big)=\{0\}$ we get by the decomposition \eqref{eq_decHf} that
\begin{equation*}
\mathscr{v}=\operatorname{rank}\big(\mathrm d_0(\xi)\big)=\operatorname{rank} \big(\mathrm d_0(\xi)^*\mathrm d_0(\xi)\big) = \operatorname{rank}\big(-\Delta_\mathcal V(\xi)\big)
\end{equation*}
and 
\begin{equation*}
\mathscr{f}=\operatorname{rank}\big(\mathrm d_1(\xi)^*\big)=\operatorname{rank}\big(\mathrm d_1(\xi)\mathrm d_1(\xi)^*\big) = \operatorname{rank}\big(-\Delta_\mathcal F(\xi)\big),
\end{equation*}
and hence the equalities in \eqref{eq_with0} hold without the need of subtracting $\{0 \}$. 

On the other hand, if $\operatorname{Ker}\big(-\Delta_\mathcal A(\xi)\big)\neq \{0\}$, we infer by a dimensional argument that
$\operatorname{rank}\big(\mathrm d_0(\xi)\big)< \mathscr{v} $ or $\operatorname{rank}\big(\mathrm d_1(\xi)\big)^*<\mathscr{f}$, or both if the degeneracy is of greater multiplicity. 
In both cases, it means that $0$ belongs to all sets (or union of sets) in \eqref{eq_with0},
and therefore these equalities again hold without the need of subtracting $\{0 \}$. This directly leads to the statement.

\label{theo:Dm}
For any $m\geq 0$ one has 
\begin{equation*}
\sigma(D_m) =\bigcup_{\xi\in\mathbb{T}^2}\sigma\big(D_m(\xi)\big)
\end{equation*}
with
\begin{align*}
\sigma\big(D_m(\xi)\big)
&= \Big\{-\sqrt{\sigma\big(-\Delta_\mathcal V(\xi)\big)+m^2}\Big\}
\cup \Big\{\sqrt{\sigma\big(-\Delta_\mathcal V(\xi)\big)+m^2}\Big\}\\
&\quad \cup \Big\{-\sqrt{\sigma\big(-\Delta_\mathcal F(\xi)\big)+m^2}\Big\}
\cup \Big\{\sqrt{\sigma\big(-\Delta_\mathcal F(\xi)\big)+m^2}\Big\}\ .
\end{align*}
In addition, $D_m$ has a flat band if and only if $-\Delta_\mathcal V$ or $-\Delta_\mathcal F$ have one.

For $\xi \in \mathbb{T}^d$, let $G_m(\xi)\in M_{\mathscr{v}+\mathscr{e}}(\mathbb{C})$ be defined by
\begin{equation*}
G_m(\xi) :=
\begin{pmatrix}
m \, I_\mathscr{v} & \mathrm d_0(\xi)^{*}\\
\mathrm d_0(\xi) & -m \, I_\mathscr{e}
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation*}
Then, for any $m\geq 0$ one has 
\begin{equation*}
\sigma\big(G_m(\xi)\big) = \{-m\} \cup 
\Big\{-\sqrt{\sigma\big(-\Delta_\mathcal V(\xi)\big)+m^2}\Big\}
\cup \Big\{\sqrt{\sigma\big(-\Delta_\mathcal V(\xi)\big)+m^2}\Big\}
\end{equation*}
and
\begin{equation*}
\sigma(G_m)=
\bigcup_{\xi \in \mathbb{T}^2}\sigma \big(G_m(\xi)\big)
= \{-m\}\cup \Big\{-\sqrt{\sigma(-\Delta_\mathcal V)+m^2}\Big\}
\cup \Big\{\sqrt{\sigma(-\Delta_\mathcal V)+m^2}\Big\},
\end{equation*}
where the eigenvalue $-m$ has multiplicity $\mathscr{f}$.

\label{rem_invariance}
1) It is readily observed that the expressions for the matrices $\mathrm d_1(\xi)$ and
$\mathrm d_1(\xi)\mathrm d_1(\xi)^*$ depend on the choice of $\mathcal V_0$, $\mathcal A_0$, and $\mathcal F_0$.
However, the expression for the above characteristic polynomial and for the eigenvalues does not depend on this choice.
On the other hand, these expressions also depend on the choice of generators for the action
of $\mathbb{Z}^2$, but the spectrum of $-\Delta_\mathcal F$ (obtained by taking a union over all $\xi \in \mathbb{T}^2$)
does not. We refer to \cite{KR} for a precise discussion about these dependences.

2) We also note that the function
\begin{equation*}
\mathbb{T}^2\ni (\xi_1,\xi_2) \mapsto \cos(2 \pi \xi_1) + \cos(2 \pi (\xi_1 - \xi_2)) +  \cos(2 \pi \xi_2)\in \mathbb{C}
\end{equation*} 
has been studied in \cite[Sec.~3.1]{PeTa21}, and similar investigations would directly lead 
to a formula for the integrated density of states.

\label{prop_dual}
For any planar graph $X$, its Laplacian on faces $-\Delta_\mathcal F$ is unitarily equivalent to the graph-Laplacian $-\Delta_{\mathcal V^*}$ 
of its dual graph.
